---
onetaskgraph_template: 1
description: >-
  The design document a plan is read and approved as. The variables below are its data; how
  to write each answer is stated in this template's guidance comments, which no rendering
  carries.
variables:
  what:
    description: >-
      The What section's prose.
    type: text
    required: true
  why:
    description: >-
      The Why section's prose.
    type: text
    required: true
  architecture:
    description: >-
      The Architecture section's overview only. Each unit's subsection is rendered from
      `units`.
    type: text
    required: true
  units:
    description: >-
      One object per unit that changes, in reading order, with these keys: `name` (string,
      the unit's label, which a planned task's `unit` names); `repository` (string, the
      repository's short name, such as `onepipeline`); `part` (string, the package or path
      within that repository, or `""` when the unit is the whole repository); `summary`
      (text, the unit's one paragraph); `reversible` (a list of objects, the unit's trivial and
      low-cost decisions in reading order, or `[]` when it has none), each with the string
      keys `title` (its short bold title) and `text` (its one sentence); and `decisions` (a
      list of objects, the unit's high-cost decisions, or `[]` when it has none). Each decision has the keys `name`
      (string, its heading), `justification` (string, naming who would have to act to undo
      it), `summary` (text, its plain meaning) and `artifact` (text, one or more fenced
      blocks, or `""` for a behavioural decision). The template renders each decision's
      reversibility line from its `justification`; the level is not an answer.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: true
  acceptance_criteria:
    description: >-
      One criterion per entry, each a string.
    type: list
    items: string
    required: true
  planned_tasks:
    description: >-
      One object per task of the plan, in the order the table lists them, with five string
      keys: `task` (its title), `unit` (the `name` of the unit it changes), `delivers` (what
      it delivers), `depends_on` (the titles it depends on, or `none`) and `location` (where
      the task lives). Each value is rendered as one table cell on one line.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: true
---
{#- A table cell is one line holding no bare column delimiter, whatever an answer says. -#}
{% macro cell(value) %}{{ value | replace("|", "\\|") | replace("\r\n", " ") | replace("\n", " ") | replace("\r", " ") }}{% endmacro %}
{% block document_guidance %}
{#
  THE READER. A technical product manager with no depth in this domain. They read this
  document instead of the plan, and must be able to accept a hard-to-reverse decision, argue
  with the architecture, or say the acceptance criteria do not describe what was asked.

  PROSE. Plain, simple, terse and precise, and no more technical than the plan's own goal
  statement. A term that cannot be avoided is defined once, in passing.

  LENGTH. A document is about as long as the design document this template replaces would
  be for the same plan. Exactness is spent on what is hard to undo, and everything else is
  said in a sentence.

  SECTIONS. Five, in this order: What, Why, Architecture, Acceptance criteria, Planned
  tasks. There is no separate Contracts section, and no answer adds a heading of its own.

  HIGHLIGHTING. No GitHub alert blocks (`> [!CAUTION]` and the like) anywhere. The bold
  reversibility line is the whole of the highlighting.

  THIS TEMPLATE. Each section renders inside its own block: `what`, `why`, `architecture`,
  `acceptance_criteria` and `planned_tasks`. Each section's guidance is a Jinja comment in a
  separate block, `<name>_guidance`, and this block, `document_guidance`, holds what applies
  to the whole document. Guidance is always a Jinja comment, never an HTML comment, so a
  rendered document carries none of it. The front matter states the data shape, what each
  answer and key holds; the judgment lives here. A repository overrides any one block, a
  section's guidance without its rendering or the other way round, through the engine's
  repository layer: `.onepipeline/templates/design-doc.md.j2` in its checkout, a file
  beginning `{% extends "onepipeline/host/design-doc.md.j2" %}` that redefines only the
  blocks it names.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block what_guidance %}
{#
  WHAT. What is being built. What and Why are each a few plain sentences in the user's
  terms, and carry no interface detail and no implementation detail.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block what %}
## What

{{ what }}

{% endblock %}
{% block why_guidance %}
{#
  WHY. Why the user wants it, in their own words where the plan records them.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block why %}
## Why

{{ why }}

{% endblock %}
{% block architecture_guidance %}
{#
  ARCHITECTURE. The `architecture` answer is a short overview, two to four sentences saying
  how the units fit. A unit is a repository, or a part of a project. Every unit that changes
  gets one subsection, headed `### <unit> — `<repository>` (`<package or path>`)`; the
  parenthesis appears only when the unit is part of a repository.

  Under each unit heading:
    - One paragraph. It says in plain words what the unit is and what changes in it. It
      does not list the unit's individual decisions; those follow.
    - One `#### <decision>` per high-cost decision the unit owns, and only for those, so a
      unit with none has no `####`. Each opens with `**Reversibility: high cost** —` and a
      justification naming who would have to act to undo it. Then one or two plain
      sentences say what the decision means. Then the literal artifact: a `diff` block for
      a changed shape, the full shape for a new one, a `--help` diff for a CLI, a text
      sketch for a UI. A behavioural decision with no artifact says so in one sentence.
    - Then, after the high-cost subsections, a `**Reversible:**` line followed by one
      bullet per trivial or low-cost decision the unit makes: an interface, a stored shape,
      a flag, a behaviour. Each bullet is `- **<short title>.** <one sentence>`, and the
      sentence names the thing exactly (the flag, key, field, route or command). A bullet
      carries no level label, no artifact and no justification, because trivial and low
      cost read the same here. A unit with no such decision has no `**Reversible:**` line.
    - Nothing else: no Kind, Owner or Status line, no Owes or May-assume bullets, and no
      links between sections.

  REVERSIBILITY. Three levels, defined by who would have to act to undo the decision:
    - trivial: only the unit that owns it, in one ordinary change.
    - low cost: parties we control, changing together. For example, several of our
      repositories or tools updated in step, a deprecation window, or a migration of data
      we own.
    - high cost: parties we don't control or can't list, or data we can't rewrite. For
      example, a public flag or field other people script against, a released artifact, or
      stored data that older installed versions or outside readers keep reading.
  When in doubt, the stricter level. These definitions are guidance a repository may
  override by replacing this block.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block architecture %}
## Architecture

{{ architecture }}

{% for unit in units %}
### {{ unit.name }} — `{{ unit.repository }}`{% if unit.part %} (`{{ unit.part }}`){% endif %}


{{ unit.summary }}

{% for decision in unit.decisions %}
#### {{ decision.name }}

**Reversibility: high cost** — {{ decision.justification }}

{{ decision.summary }}

{% if decision.artifact %}
{{ decision.artifact }}

{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if unit.reversible %}
**Reversible:**

{% for item in unit.reversible %}
- **{{ item.title }}.** {{ item.text | replace("\n", "\n  ") }}
{% endfor %}

{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endblock %}
{% block acceptance_criteria_guidance %}
{#
  ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA. A plain list of what must be observably true when the plan is done,
  no longer than the plan warrants.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block acceptance_criteria %}
## Acceptance criteria

{% for criterion in acceptance_criteria %}
- {{ criterion | replace("\n", "\n  ") }}
{% endfor %}

{% endblock %}
{% block planned_tasks_guidance %}
{#
  PLANNED TASKS. A table with the columns Task, Unit, What it delivers, Depends on, Where it
  lives: one row per task, every cell one line, and "What it delivers" a short phrase. The
  Unit column names the unit the task changes. Where it lives is the location the plan store
  reports for the task, never one composed by hand.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block planned_tasks %}
## Planned tasks

| Task | Unit | What it delivers | Depends on | Where it lives |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
{% for row in planned_tasks %}
| {{ cell(row.task) }} | {{ cell(row.unit) }} | {{ cell(row.delivers) }} | {{ cell(row.depends_on or "none") }} | {{ cell(row.location) }} |
{% endfor %}
{% endblock %}
